rote the screenplay for the  award-winning novel's film adaptation  which itself was nominated for multiple  academy awards including best picture  as is often the case however donahue  struggled with the harsh reality that  not every scene from the book is good  for the movie  in the case of room donohue chose to cut  out the scene in which  ma tells her son jack that she gave  birth to a baby girl before him  because she had no medical attention in  captivity the child was tragically  stillborn despite having written the  scene into the script  donahue made the tough move to remove it  completely donahue told variety  the stillbirth story slammed the brakes  and yanked us back toward the tragic  past  i have to grudgingly admit that the  film's better without it  the entire conceit of the hunger games  is built on death pain torture  and cruelty in a fascist nation called  pan m an annual contest selects two  children from each of 12 districts to  fight to the death both to amuse the  elitist in charge  and to punish the population for a  failed uprising from many years earlier  i volunteer as tribute not only does  president snow's government in the  capital enable the deaths of 23 children  each year in elaborate war games but it  develops and utilizes technology to lead  to more suffering  both in and out of the hunger games  while suzanne collins very successful  hunger games books were aimed at teens  and children the movie adaptations  eliminated some of the capital's  particularly ghastly acts  for example anyone who tries to escape  pan am and gets caught as well as those  mixed up in potential  uprisings are turned into what are  called a voxes their tongues are cut out  meaning they can't talk and they're  forced into a life of slavery in the  capital capital employed hunger games  makers also utilize so-called mutations  genetically modified creatures to make  the competition even deadlier the final  three competitors in the first hunger  games novel face off with wolf type  monsters  and thanks to science each of these  so-called mutts unsettlingly bears the  face of one of the children sacrificed  earlier in the games  many of filmmaker cameron crowe's works  are based on true stories  in 1981 crow published vast times at  ridgemont high a true story and to  observe teenagers in their element the  22 year old writer pretended to be a  senior in a real california high school  that book became the basis of the 1982  classic teen movie  fast times at ridgemont high one of the  most salacious and eye-opening accounts  of teen life  ever put to film the breakout star was  future ultra serious two-time academy  award-winning actor sean penn who  starred as jeff spicoli a well-liked  good-natured surfer stoner dimwit  all in here some tasty waves cool buzz  and i'm fine  but in the books piccoli is a target of  his classmate's derision both for his  lack of intelligence and  outright cruelty her crow's account a  student's father and sister are killed  in a grisly car accident and spicoli  burst into the kid's class showing off a  newspaper article with crash photos the  kid cried  and nobody spoke to spicoli for a month  that scene didn't make it into the  mostly comedic fast times film  michael crichton's 1990 novel jurassic  park is a techno thriller detailing the  death  and destruction that occurs when the  science created dinosaur clones that  inhabit a remote theme park start  attacking as many humans as they can  one of the scariest and most memorable  scenes in the blockbuster 1993 film  adaptation is when small but deadly  velociraptors track down  kids lex and tim as they hide in a  kitchen they get out safely but a far  worse and sickening fate befalls a much  younger child in creighton's novel the  first sequences of the book in which  costa rica is plagued by a series of  mysterious lizard attacks didn't make it  into the movie those lizards are  obviously jurassic park escapees  and in one scene from the novel a nurse  discovers three little dinosaurs  attacking a baby  the 1990 film misery is an adaptation of  a stephen king novel  and it's a horror story that doesn't  involve monsters ghosts or creatures the  scary thing in misery is a human  and it's annie wilkes played by kathy  bates annie is a super fan of romance  novelist paul sheldon  played by james khan and she gets the  chance to meet him after saving him from  a car accident but when she finds out  that paul's upcoming novel will kill off  her favorite character annie's adoration  turns sinister and she holds paul  hostage in her home  forcing him to write what she wants when  annie leaves for the day  paul goes snooping in the house to learn  more about his captor upon her return  she realizes what he did and restrains  him against a bed  i know you've been out  then to ensure he doesn't try another  escape she violently and graphically  shatters his ankles with a sledgehammer  perhaps director rob reiner and  screenwriter william goldman are just  more squeamish than king  in the original book annie doesn't club  paul's ankles she amputates a foot with  an  axe and then cauterizes the bloody stump  wound with a blow torch  jiminy cricket from the 1940 film  pinocchio is one of the most memorable  characters in the vast universe of  disney animated films  and for good reason he's kind patient  and serves as the conscience of  marionette turned real boy pinocchio  writers and animators greatly  embellished and fleshed out the  character for the film based on carlo  calot's episodic fairy tale novel the  adventures of pinocchio published in  serial form in  1881 and 1882. in the source text jiminy  cricket is known only as talking cricket  and he isn't a major character early in  the story when pinocchio is left home  the talking cricket advises the boy to  head back to the safety and comfort of  home pinocchio flies into a rage over  being told what to do  and he picks up a hammer and throws it  at the